  <h2>The two questions behind every water claim</h2>
  <p>An adjuster's first job on a water claim isn't to measure the damage. It's to answer two questions:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Where did the water come from?</strong> A burst supply line, an appliance, a roof opening in a storm, rising surface water, a backed-up drain, groundwater through the foundation.</li>
    <li><strong>How long did it go on?</strong> A sudden event, or something that leaked, seeped or condensed over weeks or months.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Those answers decide which part of the policy applies. The damage estimate comes after. That's why the first conversation with your insurer, often a phone call you make while standing in wet socks, matters more than most people realize.</p>

  <h2>How the policy sorts water</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Typical treatment in a standard homeowners policy</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Sudden and accidental discharge</strong> from plumbing, heating, air conditioning or an appliance</td><td>Usually covered</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Constant or repeated seepage or leakage</strong> over weeks, months or years</td><td>Usually excluded, with an exception in many policies for hidden, unknown leaks</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Wear and tear, rust, deterioration</strong></td><td>The worn part is excluded; the resulting water damage often isn't</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Flood</strong> (surface water, rising water)</td><td>Excluded; needs separate flood insurance</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sewer or drain backup</strong></td><td>Excluded unless you added a backup endorsement</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Groundwater</strong> through foundations</td><td>Usually excluded</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Mold</strong></td><td>Often excluded or capped by a sublimit, except as part of a covered water loss</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Freezing</strong></td><td>Usually covered if you kept the heat on or shut off and drained the water while away</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Your policy's wording is what actually counts, and forms differ. More detail is in <a href="/guides/does-homeowners-insurance-cover-water-damage/">what insurance covers</a>.</p>

  <h2>The exception hidden in the seepage exclusion</h2>
  <p>The seepage exclusion is the most common reason water claims are denied, and its exception is the least understood. Widely used homeowners policy forms exclude constant or repeated seepage over a period of weeks, months or years, and older versions used a fixed period such as 14 days. But many of those same forms restore coverage when the seepage was <strong>hidden within walls or ceilings or beneath floors</strong> and <strong>unknown to everyone insured</strong> under the policy.</p>
  <p>In plain terms: a slow drip inside a wall that you couldn't have seen may be treated very differently from a slow drip under a sink you looked at every day. If your claim is questioned on seepage grounds, ask the adjuster for the exact policy language being applied, and read whether that exception appears in it.</p>

  <h2>Phrases that point toward an exclusion</h2>
  <p>None of this is about hiding anything. Misrepresenting a claim can void a policy, and it's fraud. It's about the difference between <strong>what you know</strong> and <strong>what you're guessing</strong>. People under stress tend to guess out loud, and guesses often point at exclusions that the facts don't support.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>What people often say</th><th>What it can signal</th><th>The accurate alternative, if true</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>"It's probably been leaking for a while."</td><td>Long-term seepage</td><td>"I found it on Tuesday morning when I saw water on the floor. I don't know when it started."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"I've noticed a smell for months."</td><td>Known, ongoing problem</td><td>Say only what you noticed and when. If the smell started recently, say so.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"The pipes are old."</td><td>Wear and tear</td><td>"The supply line under the sink split." Let the plumber state the cause in writing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Water came in from outside."</td><td>Flood or groundwater</td><td>Describe exactly where you saw water enter: through the ceiling, around a window, up through the floor drain.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"There's mold everywhere."</td><td>Mold exclusion or sublimit</td><td>Describe the water damage first, then the growth you've actually seen, and where.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"I meant to fix that."</td><td>Neglect</td><td>If you knew of a problem, say so honestly, and say what happened differently this time.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="notice notice-water"><strong>The rule of thumb:</strong> state what you saw, where, and when. When you don't know something, say "I don't know." Don't speculate about causes or durations; let the plumber's report and the evidence do that.</p>

  <h2>Who your adjuster is, and how they work</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Works for</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Staff field adjuster</strong></td><td>Your insurer</td><td>Visits in person. Usually handles larger or more complex claims.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Desk adjuster</strong></td><td>Your insurer</td><td>Works from photos, video calls and contractor estimates, often on a heavy caseload. What isn't photographed often doesn't get paid.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Independent adjuster</strong></td><td>An adjusting firm hired by your insurer</td><td>Inspects and writes the estimate; the insurer makes the payment decision. Common after storms.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Catastrophe adjuster</strong></td><td>Your insurer or an adjusting firm</td><td>Brought in from other regions after large events. May be unfamiliar with local prices.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Public adjuster</strong></td><td>You</td><td>Licensed in most states, paid a percentage of your claim. See <a href="/guides/storm-chasers-deductible-offers-public-adjusters/">when a public adjuster helps</a>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Every adjuster on the insurer's side works within an <strong>authority limit</strong>: the amount they can approve without a supervisor. If your claim stalls just above a round number, it may be waiting on a higher level of approval. It's fair to ask, "Is this within your authority, or does someone else need to approve it?"</p>

  <h2>The recorded statement</h2>
  <p>Most policies require you to cooperate with the investigation. That can include a recorded statement and, in disputed claims, an examination under oath. Cooperating doesn't mean improvising.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Schedule it.</strong> You can usually ask for a time when you have your policy, photos and notes in front of you.</li>
    <li><strong>Write a short timeline first:</strong> when you found the water, what you did, who you called, and when.</li>
    <li><strong>Answer the question asked.</strong> Short, factual answers are fine.</li>
    <li><strong>Say "I don't know" or "I'm not sure"</strong> whenever that's the truth. It's a complete answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for a copy</strong> of the recording or transcript, and correct any mistakes in writing afterward.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Evidence that matters more than any phrase</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The plumber's invoice with a stated cause,</strong> such as "burst supply line" or "pipe split due to freezing." Ask the plumber to write it on the invoice.</li>
    <li><strong>Dated photos and video</strong> of the water, the source and every affected room, taken before anything is moved.</li>
    <li><strong>The failed part.</strong> Keep the split hose, cracked fitting or failed valve in a labeled bag. Your insurer may want to inspect it, and if a defective product caused the leak, the insurer may pursue the manufacturer. Throwing it away can complicate both.</li>
    <li><strong>Moisture logs</strong> from the restoration company, showing the extent of the water. See <a href="/guides/moisture-meters-thermal-cameras-is-it-dry/">is it actually dry</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Receipts</strong> for emergency expenses: towels, fans, a hotel night, the plumber.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your duty to limit the damage</h2>
  <p>Policies require you to take reasonable steps to protect the property from further damage: stop the water, start drying, cover openings. Damage that happens because nobody acted can be reduced or denied. That's why waiting for the adjuster before calling anyone to dry the house is usually a mistake. See <a href="/guides/call-insurance-or-restoration-company-first/">who to call first</a>.</p>

  <h2>Letters you may receive</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reservation of rights:</strong> the insurer is investigating and may later decide some or all of the claim isn't covered. It isn't a denial. Ask which provisions it's reserving rights under.</li>
    <li><strong>Request for proof of loss:</strong> a sworn statement of what you're claiming, often with a deadline. Don't miss it; ask for an extension in writing if you need one.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial denial:</strong> some items paid, some not. Ask for the reason for each denied item, with the policy language, in writing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How you're paid: ACV and depreciation</h2>
  <p>Many policies first pay <strong>actual cash value</strong>: the replacement cost minus depreciation. The withheld depreciation is often <strong>recoverable</strong> once repairs are done or items are replaced, usually within a deadline set by the policy. Keep every receipt, and diary the deadline.</p>

  <h2>If the claim goes wrong</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask for every decision and its reason in writing.</li>
    <li>Escalate to the adjuster's supervisor.</li>
    <li>Contact your state insurance department's consumer line about claim handling.</li>
    <li>Check your policy's appraisal clause for disputes over the amount of loss.</li>
    <li>For large or denied claims, consider a licensed public adjuster or an attorney.</li>
  </ul>

